The Biographies of Zhang Shizhi and Feng Tang

Zhang Shizhi, Commandant of Justice, was a man of Duyang, courtesy name Ji. He had an elder brother Zhong with whom he lived together. By means of his wealth he became a mounted attendant and served Emperor Xiaowen; for ten years he was not transferred, and he was nowhere known. Shizhi said: "Long service in office has diminished Zhong's property, and I have not succeeded." He wished to resign and return home. The General of the Household Gentlemen, Yuan Ang, knowing his worth, regretted his departure, and so requested that Shizhi be transferred to fill the post of Receptionist. After Shizhi had finished his audience, he took the occasion to speak first of matters convenient and beneficial. Emperor Wen said: "Keep it low, do not discourse too highly; make it something that can be put into practice today." Thereupon Shizhi spoke of affairs between Qin and Han, of why Qin lost and why Han rose, for a long time. Emperor Wen praised him, and so appointed Shizhi as Chief of the Receptionists.

Shizhi accompanied the emperor on an outing and ascended the Tiger Enclosure. The emperor asked the Warden of the Shanglin Park about the registers of the various birds and beasts; after more than ten questions, the warden looked left and right and could not answer at all. The keeper of the tiger enclosure, from beside him, answered in place of the warden concerning the registers of birds and beasts about which the emperor asked, in great detail, wishing to display his ability to answer orally like an echo, inexhaustibly. Emperor Wen said: "Should officials not be like this? The warden is useless!" He then ordered Shizhi to appoint the keeper as Prefect of Shanglin. Shizhi after a long while came forward and said: "Does Your Majesty consider Marquis Jiang, Zhou Bo, what sort of man?" The emperor said: "An elder." He asked again: "Marquis Dongyang, Zhang Xiangru, what sort of man?" The emperor again said: "An elder." Shizhi said: "Now Marquis Jiang and Marquis Dongyang are called elders, yet when these two men speak of affairs they can scarcely get the words out of their mouths—how much less should one imitate this keeper, chattering and glib, quick and ready! Moreover, Qin employed clerks of the knife and brush, and the officials vied with one another in haste and harshness as a mark of superiority, but their defect was merely empty forms, without the reality of compassion. For this reason his faults were not heard, and decline continued until the Second Generation, when the world fell apart. Now Your Majesty, because of a keeper's eloquence, would promote him out of turn; I fear the world will follow the wind and fall over itself, vying in eloquence without substance. Moreover, the transformation of the people by the ruler is faster than shadow and echo; every act and omission must be carefully weighed." Emperor Wen said: "Good." So he stopped and did not appoint the keeper.

The emperor got into the carriage and summoned Shizhi to ride as companion; going slowly, he asked Shizhi about the ills of Qin. Shizhi answered fully with substantive words. When they reached the palace, the emperor appointed Shizhi as Prefect of the Palace Gates.

After a short while, the Crown Prince and the King of Liang rode together in a carriage into court and did not descend at the Sima Gate. Thereupon Shizhi pursued and stopped the Crown Prince and the King of Liang, not permitting them to enter the palace gate. He then impeached them for not descending at the public gate, an act of disrespect, and memorialized it. Empress Dowager Bo heard of it, and Emperor Wen, removing his cap, apologized, saying: "I did not teach my sons carefully." Empress Dowager Bo then sent an envoy with an imperial decree to pardon the Crown Prince and the King of Liang; only then were they able to enter. Emperor Wen from this regarded Shizhi as extraordinary and appointed him Grand Master of the Palace.

After a short while, he reached General of the Household Gentlemen. On an outing they came to Baling, and the emperor sat on the north side facing the privy. At this time Lady Shen was in attendance; the emperor pointed out to Lady Shen the road to Xinfeng, saying: "This is the road that goes to Handan." He had Lady Shen play the se, and the emperor himself leaned on the se and sang, his mood sad and sorrowful; turning to the assembled ministers he said: "Alas! If one made the coffin with the stones of the northern mountain, and used hemp and silk to cut and bind it, and stuffed it with lacquer and silk in between, could it be moved!" Those about him all said: "Good." Shizhi came forward and said: "If there is that within which may be desired, though one smelted the southern mountain it would still have crevices; if there is nothing within which may be desired, though there were no stone coffin, what would there be to grieve about!" Emperor Wen praised him. Afterwards he appointed Shizhi Commandant of Justice.

After a short while, the emperor went out and passed over the Middle Wei Bridge. A man came out from under the bridge, and the carriage horses were startled. Thereupon mounted guards were sent to arrest him, and he was handed over to the Commandant of Justice. Shizhi tried and questioned him. He said: "I am a man of the county; I came and heard the imperial clearing of the road, and hid under the bridge. After a long while, thinking the procession had already passed, I came out, and seeing the imperial carriage and riders, I ran away—that is all." The Commandant of Justice memorialized that by the Qin statute, one man who violated the clearing of the road should be fined. Emperor Wen angrily said: "This man personally startled my horses; my horses relied on their gentleness—had it been other horses, would they not surely have injured me? Yet the Commandant of Justice sentences him only to a fine!" Shizhi said: "The law is what the Son of Heaven shares in common with the world. Now if the law is thus and you change it to make it heavier, this will make the law untrustworthy among the people. Moreover, at the time it happened, if Your Majesty had had him put to death on the spot, then it would have been over. Now since he has been handed down to the Commandant of Justice—the Commandant of Justice is the balance of the world; if once it is tipped, then the application of law throughout the world will all be made lighter or heavier, and where will the people be able to place their hands and feet? I beg Your Majesty to consider it." After a long while, the emperor said: "The judgment of the Commandant of Justice is correct."

Afterwards someone stole the jade ring before the seat in the Temple of the High Ancestor; he was caught, and Emperor Wen was angry and handed him down to the Commandant of Justice for judgment. Shizhi, according to the statute on those who steal sacrificial vessels of the ancestral temple, memorialized, and the judgment was exposure in the marketplace. The emperor was greatly angry and said: "This man is without the Way—he even stole the vessels of the Former Emperor. I handed him to the Commandant of Justice because I wished to have him executed with his clan, yet you memorialize according to law—this is not what I meant in sharing in the service of the ancestral temple." Shizhi, removing his cap and bowing his head, apologized and said: "The law is thus, and it is enough. Moreover, though the crimes be equal, yet there is a difference according to whether they are against the order or not. Now if one who steals ancestral temple vessels is clanned, suppose—by a chance in ten thousand—that some foolish people took a handful of earth from the Changling tomb, how would Your Majesty add to their law?" After a long while, Emperor Wen spoke of it to the Empress Dowager, and then permitted the judgment of the Commandant of Justice. At this time, the Commandant of the Capital, Marquis Tiao Zhou Yafu, and the Chancellor of Liang, Marquis Shandu Wang Tiankai, seeing that Shizhi held his opinions fairly, formed ties of friendship with him. Zhang, the Commandant of Justice, was from this praised by the world.

Afterwards Emperor Wen died and Emperor Jing ascended the throne. Shizhi was afraid and pleaded illness. He wished to resign and leave, but feared great punishment would come; he wished to appear and apologize, but did not know how it would be. Using Wang Sheng's plan, he at last appeared and apologized, and Emperor Jing did not hold him to it.

Wang Sheng was a man of retirement who was good at the words of Huang-Lao. He had once been summoned to reside in the court; the Three Dukes and Nine Ministers all assembled and stood, and Wang Sheng, an old man, said, "My stockings have come undone," and turning to Zhang, the Commandant of Justice, said: "Tie my stockings for me!" Shizhi knelt and tied them. When it was done, someone said to Wang Sheng: "Why alone did you humiliate Zhang, the Commandant of Justice, in the court, making him kneel and tie your stockings?" Wang Sheng said: "I am old and moreover lowly, and I myself judged that in the end I would be of no benefit to Zhang, the Commandant of Justice. Zhang, the Commandant of Justice, is at present a famous minister of the world; therefore I deliberately put him to shame in the court, making him kneel and tie my stockings, wishing thereby to make him weighty." When the various gentlemen heard this, they esteemed Wang Sheng as worthy and regarded Zhang, the Commandant of Justice, the more highly.

Zhang, the Commandant of Justice, served Emperor Jing for more than a year, and became Chancellor of Huainan, still because of his former fault. After a long while, Shizhi died. His son was Zhang Zhi, courtesy name Changgong, who rose in office to Grand Master and was dismissed. Because he could not accommodate himself to the age, he never held office his whole life.

Feng Tang: his grandfather was a man of Zhao. His father moved to Dai. When Han arose, they moved to Anling. Tang was known for filial piety and became Chief of the Gentlemen's Bureau, serving Emperor Wen. Emperor Wen, riding in his carriage, passed by and asked Tang: "Old sir, how did you come to be a gentleman? Where is your home?" Tang answered fully with the facts. Emperor Wen said: "When I dwelt in Dai, my chief of provisions, Gao Qu, repeatedly spoke to me of the worth of the Zhao general Li Qi, who fought at the foot of Julu. Now whenever I eat, my thoughts are never absent from Julu. Do you know him, old sir?" Tang replied: "He was not even the equal of Lian Po and Li Mu as generals." The emperor said: "Why?" Tang said: "When my grandfather was in Zhao, he was a general of the officers and was friendly with Li Mu. My father was formerly Chancellor of Dai and was friendly with the Zhao general Li Qi, and knew what sort of man he was." When the emperor had heard what sort of men Lian Po and Li Mu were, he was greatly pleased, and slapping his thigh said: "Alas! If only I could have Lian Po and Li Mu as my generals in my time, would I worry about the Xiongnu!" Tang said: "Ruler and subject! Even if Your Majesty had Lian Po and Li Mu, you could not employ them." The emperor was angry, rose and went into the forbidden palace. After a long while, he summoned Tang and reproached him, saying: "Why do you shame me before the crowd? Is there no place apart?" Tang apologized and said: "This rustic man does not know taboo."

At this time, the Xiongnu had newly made a great incursion at Chaona and killed the Commandant of Beidi, Ang. The emperor was concerned about the barbarian raids, and so at last again asked Tang: "How do you know that I cannot employ Lian Po and Li Mu?" Tang replied: "I have heard that when the kings of antiquity sent out generals, they knelt and pushed the wheel, saying: 'Within the threshold, I, the few, control it; beyond the threshold, the general controls it.' Military merit, ranks, and rewards were all decided abroad, and on return they were reported. This was no empty saying. My grandfather said that Li Mu, as general of Zhao, held the frontier; the revenues of the military markets he used all himself to feast the soldiers, and rewards and gifts were decided abroad, without interference from within. He entrusted them and demanded success, and so Li Mu was able to exhaust his wisdom and strength. He sent out chosen chariots thirteen hundred, bowmen and cavalry thirteen thousand, and men of a hundred gold a hundred thousand; therefore he drove back the Chanyu in the north, broke the Eastern Hu, destroyed the Danlin, checked strong Qin in the west, and supported Han and Wei in the south. At that time Zhao was almost hegemon. Afterwards it happened that King Qian of Zhao came to the throne; his mother was a singer. When King Qian came to the throne, he used Guo Kai's slander and at last executed Li Mu, and had Yan Ju replace him. Therefore his soldiers were broken and his officers defeated, and he was captured and destroyed by Qin. Now I have privately heard that Wei Shang is commandant of Yunzhong; the revenues of his military markets he uses all to feast the soldiers, and he brings out his private money, and every five days he beats a bull to feast the guests, the military officers, and the retainers. Therefore the Xiongnu avoid him far off and do not approach the passes of Yunzhong. Once the barbarians did enter, and Shang led chariots and cavalry to strike them, and killed many of them. Now the soldiers are all sons of common families, risen from the fields to follow the army—how should they know the foot registers and the five-man tallies? All day they fight with strength, beheading and capturing prisoners, and when they report merit at headquarters, if a single word does not match, the civil officials bind them by law. Their rewards are not given, while the officials' application of the law is certain. I am foolish and think Your Majesty's law is too clear, your rewards too light, and your punishments too heavy. Moreover, the commandant of Yunzhong, Wei Shang, has been sentenced for the discrepancy of six heads in his report of merit; Your Majesty handed him down to the officials, stripped his rank, and punished him with labor. From this speaking, even if Your Majesty had Lian Po and Li Mu, you could not employ them. I am truly foolish and have touched on taboo; death, death!" Emperor Wen was pleased. That day he ordered Feng Tang to bear the tally and pardon Wei Shang, and restored him as commandant of Yunzhong; and he appointed Tang as Commandant of Chariots and Cavalry, in charge of the Commandant of the Capital and the chariot soldiers of the commanderies and kingdoms.

In the seventh year, Emperor Jing ascended the throne and made Tang Chancellor of Chu; he was dismissed. When Emperor Wu ascended the throne, he sought out worthy and good men, and Feng Tang was recommended. Tang was then over ninety and could no longer hold office, so his son Feng Sui was made a Gentleman. Sui, courtesy name Wangsun, was also an extraordinary man and was friendly with me.

The Grand Historian says: Zhang Ji's words on elders, and his holding the law without bending to the ruler's will; Feng Gong's discourse on generals—how full of flavor! How full of flavor! The saying goes, "If you do not know the man, look at his friends." What these two gentlemen praised and recited may be displayed in the galleries and temples. The Documents says: "Not partial, not partisan, the kingly way is wide and smooth; not partisan, not partial, the kingly way is easy and plain." Zhang Ji and Feng Gong come near to this. [end of text]


